"""Earth science, climate, geography and nature intents."""

ROWS = [
    ("climate change", "Climate change is the long-term warming of the planet, driven mainly by carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases released by burning fossil fuels. Global average temperature is already roughly 1.2 °C above the pre-industrial baseline and rising, with more heat waves, heavier floods, and rising seas as consequences. 🌍", {"asks": ["what is global warming", "why is the climate changing", "is climate change real", "what causes climate change"]}),
    ("the greenhouse effect", "The greenhouse effect is how certain gases — CO2, methane, water vapor — trap some of Earth's outgoing heat, keeping the planet about 33 °C warmer than it would otherwise be. It is natural and necessary; the problem is that humans have intensified it. 🌫️", {"keys": ["greenhouse gases"]}),
    ("the ozone layer", "The ozone layer is a band of O3 in the stratosphere that absorbs most of the Sun's ultraviolet radiation, protecting life from skin damage and mutations. It was damaged by CFCs, and the 1987 Montreal Protocol banned them — the layer is now slowly recovering. It is evidence that global cooperation can work. 🛡️", {"keys": ["ozone"]}),
    ("an earthquake", "An earthquake is a sudden release of energy when rocks along a fault slip — the energy radiates as seismic waves that shake the ground. Magnitude measures energy released (like the moment magnitude scale); intensity measures how hard a place was shaken. 🌐", {"keys": ["earthquakes"], "asks": ["what causes earthquakes", "how are earthquakes measured"]}),
    ("a volcano", "A volcano is an opening where magma, gas, and ash from inside the Earth reach the surface. Most sit along tectonic plate boundaries — the Pacific \"Ring of Fire\" hosts about 75% of the world's active volcanoes. 🌋", {"keys": ["volcanoes", "volcanic eruption"], "asks": ["how do volcanoes erupt"]}),
    ("a hurricane", "A hurricane is a powerful tropical storm with winds above 119 km/h that forms over warm ocean water. The same phenomenon is called a typhoon in the Northwest Pacific and a cyclone in the Indian Ocean and Australia. 🌀", {"keys": ["hurricanes", "typhoon", "cyclone"], "asks": ["what is a typhoon", "how do hurricanes form"]}),
    ("a tornado", "A tornado is a violently rotating column of air touching both a cloud and the ground, with winds that can exceed 300 km/h. The US Midwest's \"Tornado Alley\" sees most of them; the Enhanced Fujita (EF) scale rates their strength. 🌪️", {"keys": ["tornadoes"]}),
    ("a tsunami", "A tsunami is a series of long, fast ocean waves usually triggered by undersea earthquakes, landslides, or volcanic eruptions. In deep water the wave is barely noticeable; near shore it can rise into walls of water — which is why warning systems matter more than anything else. 🌊", {"keys": ["tsunamis"], "asks": ["what causes a tsunami"]}),
    ("plate tectonics", "Plate tectonics is the theory that Earth's outer shell is broken into moving plates that drift on the mantle. Their collisions build mountains, their separations open oceans, and their slips cause earthquakes — and they explain why the continents fit together like puzzle pieces. 🌏", {"asks": ["how do tectonic plates move", "what is continental drift"]}),
    ("the ocean", "The ocean covers about 71% of Earth's surface and holds 97% of its water. Its average depth is around 3.7 km, and the deepest point — the Mariana Trench — plunges nearly 11 km. It has absorbed roughly 90% of the extra heat from global warming, at a cost to marine life. 🌊", {"keys": ["oceans"]}),
    ("the pacific ocean", "The Pacific is Earth's largest and deepest ocean, covering about a third of the planet's surface. Its ring of plate boundaries fuels the \"Ring of Fire,\" home to most of the world's earthquakes and volcanoes. 🌊", {"keys": ["pacific"]}),
    ("the atlantic ocean", "The Atlantic is the second-largest ocean, stretching between the Americas and Europe/Africa. It is still widening by about 2–3 cm per year as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge pushes the plates apart. 🌊", {"keys": ["atlantic"]}),
    ("tides", "Tides are the rise and fall of sea levels caused mainly by the Moon's gravity, with a smaller assist from the Sun. Most coasts get two high and two low tides a day; spring tides near new and full moons are the biggest. 🌕", {"asks": ["what causes tides", "why does the sea rise and fall"]}),
    ("the seasons", "The seasons are caused by Earth's 23.5° axial tilt, not by distance from the Sun — in fact, Earth is closest to the Sun in January. The tilt changes how directly sunlight hits each hemisphere through the year. 🍂", {"asks": ["why do we have seasons", "what causes seasons"]}),
    ("the water cycle", "The water cycle is the endless circulation of water: evaporation from oceans and lakes, condensation into clouds, precipitation as rain or snow, and collection back into rivers and groundwater. The same water has been cycling for billions of years. 💧", {"asks": ["how does rain work", "where does rain come from"]}),
    ("a rainbow", "A rainbow forms when sunlight enters water droplets, bends (refracts), reflects off the back, and bends again — splitting white light into its colors at about 42° from the direction opposite the Sun. That is why it is always opposite the Sun, and why the arc is a circle you usually see only half of. 🌈", {"keys": ["rainbows"]}),
    ("lightning", "Lightning is a giant electrostatic discharge — charge builds up in storm clouds until it jumps, heating the air channel to around 30,000 °C, five times hotter than the Sun's surface. Thunder is the shockwave from that sudden heating. ⚡", {"asks": ["what causes lightning", "how hot is lightning", "why is there thunder"]}),
    ("why the sky is blue", "Sunlight scatters off air molecules, and short blue wavelengths scatter far more than red ones (Rayleigh scattering) — so blue light reaches your eyes from every direction, not just from the Sun. At sunset, light travels a longer path and the blue is scattered away, leaving reds and oranges. 🌇", {"keys": ["sky blue", "blue sky"], "asks": ["why is the sky blue", "why is the sunset red", "why are sunsets orange"]}),
    ("the equator", "The equator is the imaginary line at 0° latitude circling Earth's middle, dividing north from south. It gets the most direct sunlight year-round, so it is warm and wet — and it is where tropical rainforests and the fastest orbital speed (about 1,670 km/h) live. 🌍", {"art": ""}),
    ("latitude and longitude", "Latitude measures how far north or south you are from the equator (0° to 90°), while longitude measures east or west from the prime meridian in Greenwich (0° to 180°). Together they name any point on Earth — the coordinate system GPS builds on. 🧭", {"keys": ["coordinates"]}),
    ("time zones", "Time zones exist because Earth rotates: noon arrives at different moments at different longitudes. The world is divided into roughly 24 zones of one hour each, referenced to UTC — and some countries, like China and India, use a single wide zone for practical reasons. 🕐", {"keys": ["timezone"]}),
    ("mount everest", "Mount Everest is the highest point above sea level: 8,848.86 meters, on the border of Nepal and China. It keeps growing a few millimeters a year as the Indian plate pushes into Asia — the same collision that built the entire Himalaya. 🏔️", {"keys": ["everest", "highest mountain"], "asks": ["how tall is mount everest", "what is the tallest mountain"]}),
    ("the amazon", "The Amazon is both the world's largest river by water volume — discharging more than the next seven rivers combined — and the largest rainforest, home to roughly 10% of all known species. It is also a vast carbon store, and deforestation threatens to flip parts of it from carbon sink to source. 🌳", {"keys": ["amazon rainforest", "amazon river"]}),
    ("the sahara", "The Sahara is the largest hot desert on Earth, covering about 9 million square kilometers of North Africa — roughly the size of the United States. It was green savanna only a few thousand years ago, and its dust fertilizes the Amazon across the Atlantic. 🏜️", {"keys": ["sahara desert"]}),
    ("the seven continents", "The seven continents are Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Europe, North America, Oceania/Australia, and South America. Asia holds about 60% of humanity; Antarctica hosts zero permanent residents. 🌐", {"keys": ["continents"], "asks": ["how many continents are there", "what are the continents"]}),
    ("africa", "Africa is the second-largest continent, with 54 countries and 1.4+ billion people — the youngest population on Earth by median age. It is also the birthplace of Homo sapiens and holds the Nile and the Sahara. 🌍", {"keys": ["africa continent"]}),
    ("asia", "Asia is the largest continent, home to about 60% of the world's people, from the Middle East to Japan and Siberia. It contains both the highest point on Earth (Everest) and the lowest land (the Dead Sea shore). 🌏", {"keys": ["asia continent"]}),
    ("europe", "Europe is a continent of about 44 countries west of Asia, with roughly 750 million people. It is a peninsula of peninsulas — and note that the European Union and the continent are different things: not every European country is in the EU. 🇪🇺", {"keys": ["europe continent"]}),
    ("australia", "Australia is both a country and a continent — the only place where that is true for a single nation. Its wildlife evolved in isolation: marsupials like kangaroos and koalas, plus the Great Barrier Reef, the largest living structure on Earth. 🦘", {"keys": ["australia continent"]}),
    ("antarctica", "Antarctica is the coldest, driest, windiest continent — a desert of ice covering about 14 million square kilometers. It holds roughly 60% of the world's fresh water as ice, and no country owns it: the Antarctic Treaty reserves it for science. 🐧", {"keys": ["antarctic"]}),
    ("the arctic", "The Arctic is the northern polar region — an ocean covered by drifting sea ice, not a continent. It is warming about four times faster than the global average, and its summer sea ice has been shrinking for decades. 🐻‍❄️", {"keys": ["arctic region"]}),
    ("japan", "Japan is an island country in East Asia with about 125 million people, built across an archipelago of nearly 14,000 islands. It sits on several tectonic plates, which gives it volcanoes, hot springs, and frequent earthquakes. 🗾", {"keys": ["japan country"]}),
    ("indonesia", "Indonesia is the world's largest archipelago: more than 17,000 islands, over 270 million people, and 700+ languages. It sits on the Ring of Fire, spans three time zones, and is building a new capital, Nusantara, on Borneo while Jakarta remains the seat of government during the transition. 🇮🇩", {"keys": ["indonesia country", "nusantara"]}),
    ("the united states", "The United States is a federation of 50 states plus the District of Columbia, home to about 335 million people. It spans six time zones, from Atlantic to Hawaii-Aleutian (plus territories), and by area is the third- or fourth-largest country depending on how you count. 🇺🇸", {"keys": ["usa", "america"]}),
    ("india", "India is the world's most populous country — over 1.4 billion people — and the largest democracy by voter count. Its tech industry, space program, and film industry each operate at a scale that shapes global markets. 🇮🇳", {"keys": ["india country"]}),
    ("china", "China is the second-most-populous country (about 1.41 billion) and the world's second-largest economy. It is roughly as wide as the continental US yet uses a single official time zone. 🇨🇳", {"keys": ["china country"]}),
    ("a desert", "A desert is a region that receives very little precipitation — under about 250 mm a year — which makes it dry, not necessarily hot. Antarctica is technically the largest desert on Earth; the Sahara is the largest hot one. 🏜️", {"keys": ["deserts"]}),
    ("a rainforest", "A rainforest is a dense forest with high rainfall, found near the equator. Its layered canopy hosts extraordinary biodiversity — the Amazon alone holds about one in ten known species — and the trees recycle their own moisture, generating rain. 🌴", {"keys": ["rainforests", "jungle"]}),
    ("coral reefs", "Coral reefs are living structures built by tiny animals called polyps that secrete limestone. They host about 25% of marine species while covering under 1% of the ocean floor — and warming water makes corals expel their algae, a bleaching that can kill a reef. 🐠", {"keys": ["coral reef", "coral bleaching"]}),
    ("bees", "Bees are the superstar pollinators: they visit flowers for nectar and carry pollen between plants, enabling roughly a third of the food crops humans eat. A honeybee colony can contain 50,000 bees with one queen and an elaborate division of labor. 🐝", {"keys": ["bee", "honeybees"]}),
    ("trees", "A tree is a tall plant with a woody trunk that can live for centuries — some bristlecone pines are over 4,000 years old. Trees store carbon, produce oxygen, cool cities, and quietly run the most patient engineering project on the planet. 🌳", {"keys": ["forest", "forests", "deforestation"]}),
    ("renewable energy", "Renewable energy comes from sources that replenish themselves: sunlight, wind, water, geothermal heat, and biomass. Solar and wind costs have fallen by roughly 90% and 70% respectively over the past decade, making variability and storage — not price — the main challenge now. ☀️", {"keys": ["solar power", "wind power", "clean energy"], "asks": ["are solar panels worth it"]}),
    ("fossil fuels", "Fossil fuels — coal, oil, and natural gas — are the buried remains of ancient life, concentrated over hundreds of millions of years. Burning them releases the energy that once powered those organisms, plus the carbon they stored, which is why they drive climate change. 🛢️", {"keys": ["coal", "oil", "natural gas"]}),
    ("recycling", "Recycling turns used materials into new products, saving energy and raw materials — recycling aluminum takes about 95% less energy than making it from ore. But it is not magic: reduce and reuse come first, and not everything can be recycled everywhere. ♻️", {"asks": ["why is recycling important", "does recycling work"]}),
    ("weather and climate", "Weather is the short-term state of the atmosphere — today's rain, tomorrow's sun. Climate is the long-term statistics of that weather over decades. One cold week does not refute a warming climate, just as one hot day does not prove it. 🌦️", {"keys": ["weather vs climate", "difference between weather and climate"]}),
    ("air pollution", "Air pollution is contamination of the atmosphere by particles and gases — from vehicles, industry, fires, and more. It causes millions of premature deaths annually, mostly through fine particles (PM2.5) that reach deep into the lungs and bloodstream. 😷", {"keys": ["pollution", "smog"]}),
    ("an earthquake early warning", "Earthquake early warning systems detect the fast, harmless seismic waves that arrive first and send an alert ahead of the slower, damaging waves — buying seconds to stop trains, halt surgery, or duck under a desk. Japan's system has been running publicly since 2007. ⏱️"),
]
